  "close_rendering": "In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.",
  "whats_happening": "John opens his Gospel (his account of Jesus' life) not with a birth or a family tree but before the world exists, using the same first words as the opening of Genesis. Three short parts each use the same verb: the Word already was, the Word was with God, and the Word was God. In the third part of the verse, the Greek has no 'the' before 'God', and readers disagree about what that means.",
  "key_words": [
    {
      "surface": "λόγος",
      "lemma": "λόγος",
      "gloss": "word",
      "note": "A common Greek noun for a spoken word, an account, or a reason or principle behind things. Greek philosophers and Jewish writings about wisdom had both used it before John.",
      "strongs": "G3056"
    },
    {
      "surface": "ἦν",
      "lemma": "εἰμί",
      "gloss": "was",
      "note": "'Was' describes something already going on, not something that began. Verse 3 uses a different verb for created things: they 'came into being'.",
